tart":{"line":28,"column":3,"offset":1390},"end":{"line":30,"column":17,"offset":1546}}},{"type":"mdxJsxFlowElement","name":"Requirement","attributes":[{"type":"mdxJsxAttribute","name":"title","value":"Challenge 2: Testing the Failure of a Timeout","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":16,"offset":1565},"end":{"line":32,"column":69,"offset":1618}}}],"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your program should fail when a slot lower than the current one is passed.","position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":5,"offset":1625},"end":{"line":33,"column":79,"offset":1699}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":33,"column":5,"offset":1625},"end":{"line":33,"column":79,"offset":1699}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":3,"offset":1552},"end":{"line":34,"column":17,"offset":1717}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1,"offset":1369},"end":{"line":35,"column":19,"offset":1737}}}],"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1,"offset":0},"end":{"line":35,"column":19,"offset":1737}}},"raw":"import { ChallengeTitle } from \"../../../../components/Challenges/ChallengeTitle\";\r\nimport { RequirementList } from \"../../../../components/Challenges/RequirementList\";\r\nimport { Requirement } from \"../../../../components/Challenges/Requirement\";\r\n\r\n<ChallengeTitle>sBPF Assembly Timeout</ChallengeTitle>\r\n\r\nNow that you should starting to be comfortable writing Assembly, instead of using the `sol_get_clock_sysvar` try achieving the same goal by passing in the `Clock` account.\r\n\r\nCreate a program only has one instruction. It must:\r\n- Take in the `Clock` account (of size 40).\r\n- Take in a u64 (8-byte) slot in instruction data.\r\n- Use the first 8-bytes of the `Clock` account to grab the current slot.\r\n- Check if the current slot is greater than the slot passed in the instruction. If it is, return error code 1.\r\n\r\n> To get the right instruction data offset you can use our [tool](https://sbpf.xyz). Just remember to add one account and set its data length as 40! \r\n\r\nStart by installing the sBPF package like explained in the [tooling section](/en/courses/introduction-to-assembly/tooling) of the Introduction to Assembly course and build your program using the following command in your terminal:\r\n\r\n```\r\nsbpf build\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis will generate a `.so` file in your `target/deploy` folder. \r\n\r\nNow click on the `take challenge` button and drop the file there!\r\n\r\n<RequirementList>\r\n  <Requirement title=\"Challenge 1: Testing the Timeout\">\r\n    Your program should succeed when a slot higher than the current one is passed.\r\n  </Requirement>\r\n\r\n  <Requirement title=\"Challenge 2: Testing the Failure of a Timeout\">\r\n    Your program should fail when a slot lower than the current one is passed.\r\n  </Requirement>\r\n</RequirementList>","highlightedCode":{"code-0":{"lang":"text","hast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"pre","properties":{"class":"shiki Kiloshift","style":"background-color:#11141a;color:#f6f6f4","tabindex":"0"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"code","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{"class":"line"},"children":[{"type":"element","tagName":"span","properties":{},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"sbpf build"}]}]}]}]}]}}}}